dubrovnik is often called croatia's most  beautiful city  the country's most popular tourist  destination the port on the adriatic sea  has some 40 000 residents its  magnificent buildings bear witness to  the city's long history which dates back  to the 3rd century a.d  in 1979 unesco declared dubrovnik's  entire old city district a world  cultural heritage site  the city has inspired artists for  centuries like maya kovachevich who was  born here  the browning is special because it has a  strong bond between the history and  culture  when you pass the narrow street you can  see the different architecture from  different period  you can see the lots of different  foreigners coming here and by that  lots of different cultures are meeting  together  you can get a good overview of dubrovnik  by taking a walk along the almost two  kilometer long city wall  up to 25 meters high it's dotted with  defensive towers and forts  most of these fortifications were built  in the 14th century  back then dubrovnik controlled trade in  the adriatic  an elevated walk around the old city  wall is well worthwhile  people should come on the city walls  because they can see the  different  dublin from a different angle i like to  see the hidden gardens  which you cannot see when you're walking  down the street  i like to see the red roofs which is  also the one of the theme of my  paintings  the stradun is the main street in the  old city  after an earthquake destroyed parts of  dubrovnik in 1667  those areas were rebuilt in a unified  barak style  one of the most important buildings in  dubrovnik is the rector's palace built  in gothic and renaissance styles in the  15th century  it used to be the seat of the city  government  another late gothic and renaissance  building is the neighboring sponsa  palace where merchants once gathered to  do business  right next to the city wall is the  franciscan monastery striking colonnades  surround the cloister garden here the  monks grew herbs that they made into  medicines in the cloister pharmacy  there's been a pharmacy here in the  franciscan monastery since the 14th  century  what's special about it is that from the  beginning it wasn't just for the monks  it wasn't just for the city's rulers  it was for all of dubrovnik  zerch citizens rises on the outskirts of  the old city  you can reach its 400 meter summit on a  one-hour hike  or in just a few minutes with the cable  car  off the city's coast is the island of  lokrum monks settled here as early as  the 10th century  i like to come here to the walk  because  the colors are beautiful  i find the difference between the colors  of the red roofs of the old town and the  blue water and the sky  and after a day spent exploring  dubrovnik what better place to relax and  enjoy the view of the old city than  banya beach
 

